module Core

import Native

{-
   `pushBase64Groups` at sixty-four lanes - the `avx512` body of the function `Base64.yana` declares,
   beside the sixteen-lane one in `Base64.native.yana`. What the encoding is, and how the four
   six-bit windows are cut out of a group, are stated there.

   **The width the sixteen-lane body could not have**, and the reason is that body's own note: the
   shuffle that spreads each three-byte group across a 32-bit lane is one `pshufb` *within* a
   128-bit half, and at any wider register the groups straddle the halves, since three does not
   divide sixteen. At `avx512` a byte shuffle across the whole register is one `vpermb`
   (Analysis-Avx512.md §2.5), so the spread is one instruction at sixty-four lanes too - sixteen
   groups, forty-eight bytes read and sixty-four units written a turn, against four groups and
   twelve bytes.

   The alphabet is the same sixteen-entry offset table read at sixty-four indices, which is the
   table repeated to fill the register and one `vpermi2b` (§3.3, and step 10's `lookupRepeated`).
   Everything else is the baseline's arithmetic at the wider lane count.

   **Sixty-four lanes are written down rather than taken from `vectorBytes`**, because the spread
   pattern is a constant of exactly that many entries and a pattern cannot be written for a width
   that is not known where it is written. A build capped at 256 bits by `-vector-width` still has
   the registers and still runs this; what the cap decides is the *natural* width, which nothing
   here reads.

   The read is a whole register of which the last sixteen bytes are not used, so the loop stops
   sixty-four bytes before the end and the scalar groups finish the run - the baseline's own
   arrangement, at this width.
-}
@replaces
fn pushBase64Groups(bytes: [U8], url: Bool, &to: String) -> Size:
    let count = length(bytes)
    let groups = count / 3
    if groups <= 0 then return 0

    String.reserve(to, groups * 4)

    let start = to.units.length :: Size
    let wanted = start + groups * 4
    checkCondition((to.units.run.capacity :: Size) < wanted)
    to.units.length = truncate(wanted) :: Count

    let source = bytes.items
    let target = to.units.run.items + start

    -- Six bits in each byte of a lane, one mask per byte.
    let byte0 = splat(0x3f) :: Vec(U32, 16)
    let byte1 = splat(0x3f00) :: Vec(U32, 16)
    let byte2 = splat(0x3f0000) :: Vec(U32, 16)
    let byte3 = splat(0x3f000000) :: Vec(U32, 16)

    let letters = splat(51) :: Vec(U8, 64)
    let capitals = splat(26) :: Vec(U8, 64)
    let capitalSlot = splat(13) :: Vec(U8, 64)
    let offsets = base64Offsets(url)

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    let &o = 0 :: Size

    while i + 64 <= count:
        let block = vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(U8, 64)
        let word = bitcast(shuffle(block, [
            2, 1, 0, 0, 5, 4, 3, 3, 8, 7, 6, 6, 11, 10, 9, 9,
            14, 13, 12, 12, 17, 16, 15, 15, 20, 19, 18, 18, 23, 22, 21, 21,
            26, 25, 24, 24, 29, 28, 27, 27, 32, 31, 30, 30, 35, 34, 33, 33,
            38, 37, 36, 36, 41, 40, 39, 39, 44, 43, 42, 42, 47, 46, 45, 45
        ])) :: Vec(U32, 16)

        let w0 = (word `shr` (splat(18) :: Vec(U32, 16))) `and` byte0
        let w1 = (word `shr` (splat(4) :: Vec(U32, 16))) `and` byte1
        let w2 = (word `shl` (splat(10) :: Vec(U32, 16))) `and` byte2
        let w3 = (word `shl` (splat(24) :: Vec(U32, 16))) `and` byte3
        let windows = bitcast(w0 `or` w1 `or` w2 `or` w3) :: Vec(U8, 64)

        let run = select(windows .< capitals, capitalSlot, max(windows, letters) - letters)
        let text = windows + lookup(offsets, run)
        setVectorAt(target + o, bitcast(text) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 64))

        i = i + 48
        o = o + 64

    return pushBase64GroupsTail(bytes, url, target, i, o)
